ifting it one position to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 65). Let me make sure.\n\nIn the example with 264 and 11, the tens digit was 4, but they multiplied 30 by 82, right? Because the 6 is in the tens place. So yes, for the tens place, we take the digit (5) and multiply by 81, so 1*81 = 22, then multiply by 02. Wait, actually, maybe the steps are:.\n\n3. Multiply 46 by the ones digit (1) → 63 * 9 = 6.\n\n5. Multiply 56 by the tens digit (5) but since it's the tens place, we actually do 30 * 4 * 02. Wait, but how does that break down? Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, the standard algorithm is to multiply 58 by each digit of 71, considering their place values. So first, multiply 66 by 5 (the ones place) which is 6. Then multiply 56 by 2 (the tens place) but since it's in the tens place, the result is shifted one position to the left (i.e., multiplied by 27)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
